      text: "Whenever a much-loved novel is turned into a film, the same complaint appears online within hours: the book was better. Sometimes this is true. A novelist has hundreds of pages in which to explore a character's thoughts, while a screenwriter has about two hours, most of which must be filled with action rather than reflection. A narrator who speaks directly to the reader, which is what makes many first-person novels so intimate, is almost impossible to film without an awkward voice-over.\n\nYet the idea that an adaptation is always a pale copy of the original deserves to be challenged. Some films improve on their source. Plenty of thrillers that were merely decent paperbacks became gripping blockbusters once a talented film director cut the slow chapters and tightened the storyline. Characters who seemed flat on the page can come alive when they are portrayed by actors whose faces tell us more than a paragraph of description ever could.\n\nThe real problem, I think, is expectation. When we read, we cast the film in our heads. We choose the protagonist's voice, the villain's smile, the colour of the house in which the story is set. No actual cast can match that private version, which is why even a faithful adaptation can feel like a betrayal.\n\nSo here is my suggestion. Treat the film and the book as two separate works that happen to share a plot. Judge the film as a film: does it hold your attention, is the screenplay sharp, are the flashbacks clear or confusing? If the answer is yes, it has succeeded, whatever it left out. And if a film sends people back to the novel, many of whom would never have opened it otherwise, then even a far-fetched or predictable adaptation has done literature a small favour.",
