      title: "Почему песня застревает в голове",
      text: "You wake up, make coffee, and there it is again: the same eight seconds of a song you heard in a supermarket yesterday, playing on repeat inside your head. Psychologists call this an involuntary musical image, but most of us know it by a friendlier name: an earworm.\n\nEarworms are remarkably common. In one large survey, almost nine out of ten people reported having one at least once a week. Interestingly, the songs responsible are rarely the ones we consider our favourites. Having analysed thousands of reports, researchers found that the most 'sticky' tunes share certain features. They tend to be fairly fast, with a simple, catchy melody that rises and falls in predictable steps, yet they usually contain one small surprise, such as an unexpected leap or an unusual rhythm. That combination of familiar and strange seems to keep the brain busy, trying to finish a pattern it cannot quite let go of.\n\nContext matters too. Songs heard several times in a short period, for example a chart-topping single played by every radio station, are more likely to stick. So are tunes linked to strong emotions or to a particular place. Stress and tiredness make things worse, and so, oddly, does boredom: a mind with nothing to do will happily replay the chorus of an advert for hours.\n\nIs there a cure? Scientists have tested a few surprisingly simple ideas. Listening to the whole song, rather than the fragment stuck in your head, helps some people, because it allows the brain to 'complete' the music. Others find that solving a moderately difficult puzzle, such as a crossword, pushes the tune out. And in one well-known experiment, volunteers chewing gum reported fewer earworms, probably because chewing interferes with the silent 'singing' that keeps the loop going.\n\nStill, not everyone wants to get rid of them. Many musicians see earworms as a compliment. After all, writing a song that thousands of strangers cannot stop humming is exactly what most composers dream of.",
