    title: "Фестиваль, который изменил город",
    text: "Fifteen years ago, few people outside Northern Portugal had heard of Vila Nova da Serra, a small hill town whose population had been falling for decades. Young people left for Lisbon or abroad, shops closed one by one, and the beautiful old theatre in the main square, which had been built in 1912, stood empty. Today the town hosts one of the most talked-about arts festivals in Europe, attracting around forty thousand visitors every July.\n\nThe festival began almost by accident. Having returned home after years working as a stage designer in Paris, Teresa Alves persuaded a few friends from the art world to spend a week in the town, putting on free performances in the streets. The first year, the audience consisted mainly of local pensioners, some of whom, she admits, came out of curiosity rather than love of contemporary dance. But the idea caught on. Within five years, the line-up included internationally known musicians, and tickets for the main concerts were selling out months in advance.\n\nThe effects on the town have been remarkable. The theatre has been restored, three new restaurants have opened, and several families have moved back. Local craftspeople now sell their work to visitors from all over the world, and the festival employs more than two hundred people each summer.\n\nNot everyone is entirely happy, however. Some residents complain that rents have risen sharply and that, during festival week, the narrow streets become unbearably crowded. 'I'm proud of what we've achieved,' says the mayor, 'but we must be careful not to turn into a tourist trap. The festival only works because it feels authentic.' To protect that atmosphere, the organisers have limited the number of tickets and now hold some events in nearby villages, spreading both the visitors and the money a little more widely.\n\nFor Teresa Alves, the most important result is not economic. 'Art reminded people that this town is worth living in,' she says. 'That is something you cannot measure in ticket sales.'",
